WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.630 --> 00:00:03.790
Doctor Strange is a very specific kind of
superhero.

00:00:03.790 --> 00:00:08.000
Instead of patrolling the city for muggers
or bank robbers, he fights demons and warlords

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:13.700
from other dimensions and deals with the manifestations
of otherwise abstract concepts, like Eternity

00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:14.700
and Death.

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:18.730
But while his bizarre, mind-bending adventures
have made him one of the weirder characters

00:00:18.730 --> 00:00:23.810
in the Marvel Universe, the good Doctor has
left huge footprints on the cultural landscape.

00:00:23.810 --> 00:00:28.800
From Steve Ditko’s psychedelic extra-dimensional
landscapes to his numerous connections to

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:34.120
the world of rock ‘n’ roll, here’s the
truth behind Dr. Stephen Strange.

00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:36.070
Who wants to live forever?

00:00:36.070 --> 00:00:41.210
In 1974's Doctor Strange #4, the sorcerer
battles Death.

00:00:41.210 --> 00:00:42.210
Literally.

00:00:42.210 --> 00:00:46.500
Inside the Orb of Agamotto, Doctor Strange
is confronted with the character Death, the

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:51.060
same skeletal woman whose tender affections
have always been craved by Thanos.

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:55.500
Strange fights hard to free himself, but ultimately
recalls the Ancient One's teachings about

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:57.930
how death is just another part of life.

00:00:57.930 --> 00:01:01.970
After letting Death overcome him, he awakens
to find that this was merely the first in

00:01:01.970 --> 00:01:05.379
a series of trials that he must confront as
the Sorcerer Supreme.

00:01:05.379 --> 00:01:09.399
As a result of his surrender to Death, Doctor
Strange is now actually ageless.

00:01:09.399 --> 00:01:11.909
He can still die, but only from being killed
by others.

00:01:11.909 --> 00:01:16.740
As far as natural causes are concerned, he’ll
be around after most of us are dust, playing

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:18.899
cards with Thor and Wolverine.

00:01:18.899 --> 00:01:21.729
The Sorcerer Supreme vs. Amy Grant

00:01:21.729 --> 00:01:27.729
In 1986, Christian singer Amy Grant released
a greatest hits compilation titled The Collection.

00:01:27.729 --> 00:01:32.170
Four years later, a Marvel Comics artist decided
Amy Grant could look a lot like a vampire.

00:01:32.170 --> 00:01:37.310
The similarity did not go unnoticed, and Amy
Grant's managers brought Marvel Comics to

00:01:37.310 --> 00:01:38.310
court.

00:01:38.310 --> 00:01:42.609
Their complaint cited the comic's connections
to "witchcraft and the occult," and how associating

00:01:42.609 --> 00:01:47.280
Amy Grant with such a comic could do "irreparable
injury" to her reputation among Christian

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:48.280
fans.

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:52.969
The two parties settled 1991, and while we
don't know the terms of the agreement, we

00:01:52.969 --> 00:01:54.289
do know one thing.

00:01:54.289 --> 00:01:59.460
If you look up the digital version of Doctor
Strange, Sorcerer Supreme #15 today, you’ll

00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:04.119
find an empty, red background replacing Amy
Grant's borrowed face.

00:02:04.119 --> 00:02:05.130
Too many zeroes

00:02:05.130 --> 00:02:10.721
In 2012, The New York Post tried to speak
to Steve Ditko, the creator of Doctor Strange

00:02:10.721 --> 00:02:12.030
and co-creator of Spider-Man.

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:17.550
It didn’t work out, mainly because Ditko
is legendarily averse to talking to the media.

00:02:17.550 --> 00:02:22.090
He did, however, answer one question: when
they asked if he received any money for any

00:02:22.090 --> 00:02:26.030
of the Spider-Man films that had been released
up to that point, he simply said “no.”

00:02:26.030 --> 00:02:29.010
But that might not be entirely accurate.

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:34.770
In 2016, Abraham Reisman staked out Steve
Ditko's Manhattan studio, hoping to get a

00:02:34.770 --> 00:02:36.910
few words with the reclusive creator.

00:02:36.910 --> 00:02:41.760
The best he managed was to get Ditko to answer
his studio door, which Ditko promptly shut

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:46.070
again after shaking his head in what Reisman
describes as "disgust."

00:02:46.070 --> 00:02:50.240
He had more luck with other people with offices
in the building, including one unnamed woman

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:54.590
who said she’d mistakenly received a check
meant for Ditko, which she realized wasn’t

00:02:54.590 --> 00:02:57.590
hers when it had, quote, “too many zeroes.”

00:02:57.590 --> 00:03:01.380
If the story is true, that could mean that
there is some kind of an agreement between

00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:03.200
Ditko and Sony or Marvel Studios.

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:08.270
And if it’s not Spider-Man, the most likely
candidate is the Sorcerer Supreme.

00:03:08.270 --> 00:03:10.980
Doctor Strange is cool with Doctor Strange

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:14.960
While Steve Ditko may not want anything to
do with film adaptations of his creations,

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:17.740
at least one of his creations feels differently.

00:03:17.740 --> 00:03:22.520
In The Unbelievable Gwenpool #3, the title
character makes an appointment with Doctor

00:03:22.520 --> 00:03:23.520
Strange.

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:26.510
As a comic book reader from the real world
who found herself transported to the Marvel

00:03:26.510 --> 00:03:31.740
Universe, Gwen has no valid Social Security
number or other identifying information to

00:03:31.740 --> 00:03:34.030
give her new employer, meaning that she doesn’t
get paid.

00:03:34.030 --> 00:03:38.670
While it’s not Strange’s usual problem,
making fake IDs for travelers from another

00:03:38.670 --> 00:03:41.010
universe is certainly something he can do.

00:03:41.010 --> 00:03:44.980
When Strange peeks into Gwen’s native dimension,
he discovers the relationship between the

00:03:44.980 --> 00:03:49.170
real world and his own specifically the film
based on his own comics.

00:03:49.170 --> 00:03:52.840
While he might not be thrilled, he definitely
seems okay with the casting choice for the

00:03:52.840 --> 00:03:54.540
main character.

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:56.670
The Doctor ROCKS

00:03:56.670 --> 00:04:00.650
One of the first things we learn about Stephen
Strange in the 2016 film is that he has an

00:04:00.650 --> 00:04:03.030
encyclopedic knowledge of modern music.

00:04:03.030 --> 00:04:06.960
But it shouldn't have come as a surprise,
since Doctor Strange has a long history in

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:08.210
the world of rock.

00:04:08.210 --> 00:04:12.960
Judging by the covers, the song titles, and
the lyrics, his biggest impact was on Pink

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:19.100
Floyd, who used interior art from Strange
Tales #158 on the cover to their 1968 album

00:04:19.100 --> 00:04:20.760
Saucerful of Secrets.

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:22.040
It doesn't stop there.

00:04:22.040 --> 00:04:27.350
T. Rex mentioned Doctor Strange in "Mambo
Sun" on the 1971 album Electric Warrior.

00:04:27.350 --> 00:04:32.590
In 1965 Jefferson Airplane, The Charlatans,
the Great Society, and others appeared on

00:04:32.590 --> 00:04:36.810
a handbill for a concert they called "A Tribute
to Doctor Strange."

00:04:36.810 --> 00:04:40.440
The prize for the most elaborate tribute to
the Sorcerer Supreme, though, has to go to

00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:41.440
Al Stewart.

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:47.860
The album art for 1974's Past, Present and
the Future and 1975's Modern Times both feature

00:04:47.860 --> 00:04:52.190
Doctor Strange, leaping into a portal and
then appearing in a flash of light before

00:04:52.190 --> 00:04:53.700
a mansion.

00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:55.790
The clothes make the man

00:04:55.790 --> 00:05:00.850
In 1968, Doctor Strange took over Strange
Tales as his own comic, but sales began to

00:05:00.850 --> 00:05:01.850
slump.

00:05:01.850 --> 00:05:06.120
To shake things up, his costume was altered
to a more superheroic look, but the most interesting

00:05:06.120 --> 00:05:10.510
detail here isn’t the costume itself it’s
the ridiculous way it was explained.

00:05:10.510 --> 00:05:16.150
Doctor Strange #177 opens with Strange and
his lover Clea banished to another dimension

00:05:16.150 --> 00:05:20.160
by a villain who mystically disguised himself
in Strange's form.

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:24.850
When Strange attempts to re-enter his own
dimension, an unseen mystical barrier stops

00:05:24.850 --> 00:05:28.120
him because there’s already someone there
who looks like him.

00:05:28.120 --> 00:05:32.990
That's right: to fool the magical, mystical
boundaries of the universe itself, Doctor

00:05:32.990 --> 00:05:36.690
Strange casts a spell that changes his costume
and creates a mask.

00:05:36.690 --> 00:05:38.800
Because apparently that’s all it takes.

00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:41.290
Maybe he should've just tried glasses?

00:05:41.290 --> 00:05:43.270
The Substitute Supreme

00:05:43.270 --> 00:05:48.450
Before Marvel's Netflix heroes took the name,
the comic book Defenders were a very different

00:05:48.450 --> 00:05:51.840
group with lots...and lots of incarnations.

00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:56.250
While the roster would change a dozen times
over the years, Doctor Strange was almost

00:05:56.250 --> 00:06:00.710
always part of the team which is pretty ironic
since writer Roy Thomas originally didn’t

00:06:00.710 --> 00:06:02.000
want him involved at all.

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:06.970
In a 2013 interview, Thomas said he wanted
the line-up from a story he wrote called “Titans

00:06:06.970 --> 00:06:10.120
Three,” with the Hulk, Namor, and the Silver
Surfer.

00:06:10.120 --> 00:06:14.870
Unfortunately for Thomas, Stan Lee was very
protective of the Silver Surfer, and hadn't

00:06:14.870 --> 00:06:16.490
been thrilled by “Titans Three.”

00:06:16.490 --> 00:06:21.660
To match the Surfer’s cosmic power, he suggested
that Thomas use Doctor Strange instead, which

00:06:21.660 --> 00:06:25.240
is exactly what he did in Marvel Feature #1.

00:06:25.240 --> 00:06:27.660
This time, it seems Lee approved.

00:06:27.660 --> 00:06:31.720
Once the Defenders got their own title a few
months later, the Silver Surfer joined the

00:06:31.720 --> 00:06:33.590
team in #2.

00:06:33.590 --> 00:06:34.949
Galactus?

00:06:34.949 --> 00:06:36.320
Pfft.

00:06:36.320 --> 00:06:38.940
By the way, Doctor Strange is powerful.

00:06:38.940 --> 00:06:43.970
So powerful that even Galactus, the Devourer
of Worlds, is no problem at all.

00:06:43.970 --> 00:06:49.340
Well, that might be an exaggeration, but when
the Avengers and Fantastic Four combined forces

00:06:49.340 --> 00:06:54.090
against Galactus, Doctor Strange was the one
who turned the tide with a spell that froze

00:06:54.090 --> 00:06:56.090
the Devourer in his tracks.

00:06:56.090 --> 00:07:00.650
The heroes took the advantage, and once the
smoke cleared, the sorcerer revealed the spell

00:07:00.650 --> 00:07:05.370
he cast forced Galactus to confront "the ghosts
of all those he has slain."

00:07:05.370 --> 00:07:10.060
Considering Galactus has destroyed entire
worlds, it's easy to see why this particular

00:07:10.060 --> 00:07:12.210
tactic was effective.

00:07:12.210 --> 00:07:13.210
Thanks for watching!

00:07:13.210 --> 00:07:16.060
